{"qid":"Q65968","names":["House of Habsburg"],"title":"House of Habsburg","endonym":{"native":"Habsburger","lang":"de"},"capsule":"The House of Habsburg rose from a minor Alsatian comital family to become the dominant dynasty of medieval and early-modern Europe, beginning when Rudolf I was elected Holy Roman Emperor in 1273 and acquired Austria in 1278 by defeating Ottokar II of Bohemia at the Battle on the Marchfeld. The dynasty's power reached its greatest contiguous extent under Maximilian I (r. 1486–1519), who assembled the Burgundian Netherlands and arranged the dynastic marriages that brought Spain, Naples, and Bohemia-Hungary into Habsburg hands for his grandson Charles V. The dataset's terminal date of 1516 corresponds to Charles's accession in Spain, after which the composite monarchy is generally treated as the Spanish Habsburg and Austrian Habsburg branches.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. Habsburg territories are well documented through imperial records and genealogical histories, but the polity as an undivided entity exists only in the period before 1516 and the territorial representation will blend distinct feudal holdings into an approximate outline.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Wikipedia: House of Habsburg","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/House_of_Habsburg"},{"label":"Britannica: Habsburg dynasty","url":"https://www.britannica.com/topic/Habsburg-dynasty"},{"label":"Evans, R.J.W., The Making of the Habsburg Monarchy 1550–1700 (Oxford University Press, 1979) — JSTOR","url":"https://www.jstor.org/stable/j.ctt7zv35q"}],"britannica":"topic/House-of-Habsburg","cowCode":null,"wikip